Legal Judgments:
Taking interest in violation of prohibitions;
Privately employing archers (gongbing);
Postal couriers delaying beyond the schedule;
Defrauding the government or private individuals to obtain property;
Unauthorized levying of exactions on the pretext of public business.

Third Session
Five Policy Questions

Question: In antiquity, sage emperors and enlightened kings received the Mandate of Heaven to occupy the position of both ruler and teacher. Toward their officials and people, they not only led and governed them, but also had the means to instruct them; their words set forth in the Classics are numerous. We reverently reflect that the Grand Progenitor, Emperor Gao (Taizu, the Hongwu Emperor), and the Grand Ancestor, Emperor Wen (Taizong, the Yongle Emperor), succeeded to Heaven and brought forth governance, taking the heart of the ancient emperors and kings as their own heart, admonishing the officials and instructing the common people. Their sage counsels and great injunctions are set forth in three books—Grand Pronouncements (Da gao), Secret Merits of Doing Good (Wei shan yin zhi), and True Examples of Filial Piety (Xiaoshun shishi)—which have been promulgated throughout the court and the provinces, and which you gentlemen have studied and recited for a long time. It is said therein that "in antiquity, great ministers associated closely with their ruler." Seeking examples from former dynasties, among those who brought stability to the altars of soil and grain and bestowed blessings upon the living people, whose illustrious achievements are manifest and indelible for ten thousand generations, who should be ranked first? In exhausting filial piety toward one's parents, sufficient to…
